{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Improve operational visibility for inference workloads on Amazon Bedrock with new CloudWatch metrics for TTFT and Estimated Quota Consumption | Artificial Intelligence","context":"### **On-demand inference:**\n\n`EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage = InputTokenCount + CacheWriteInputTokens + (OutputTokenCount × burndown_rate)`\n\nThe burndown rate varies by model — for the full list of models and their applicable rates, refer to Token burndown multipliers for quota management. For on-demand inference, cache read tokens don’t count toward the quota.\n\nAs an example, Claude Sonnet 4.5 has a 5x burndown rate on output tokens. An on-demand request with 1,000 input tokens, 200 cache write tokens, and 100 output tokens consumes 1,000 + 200 + (100 × 5) = 1,700 tokens of quota. This is 400 more than you might estimate from raw token counts alone.\n\n**Note:** The `EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage` metric tracks your estimated Tokens Per Minute (TPM) quota consumption, including cache write tokens and output burndown multipliers. However, the `max_tokens` parameter can impact your actual quota consumption and throttling behavior. When Amazon Bedrock processes your request, it initially reserves quota capacity based on `InputTokenCount + CacheWriteInputTokens + max_tokens`. During processing, this reservation is dynamically adjusted based on actual output tokens generated. For example, if you set `max_tokens=32,000` but only generate 1,000 output tokens, your initial quota deduction is far higher than your final consumption — temporarily reducing your concurrent request capacity and potentially causing throttling even when `EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage` shows you are well below your limits. To avoid unexpected throttling, right-size your `max_tokens` parameter to match your expected output length rather than using arbitrarily high values. For models with 5x burndown rates (Anthropic Claude models version 3.7 and later), the gap between initial and final quota deductions can be large. Monitor your `InputTokenCount` and `OutputTokenCount` CloudWatch metrics to guide your `max_tokens` decisions, and vary this parameter dynamically based on request type rather than using a single high value for all scenarios. Read more on how max_tokens parameter influences token-based throttling","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/improve-operational-visibility-for-inference-workloads-on-amazon-bedrock-with-new-cloudwatch-metrics-for-ttft-and-estimated-quota-consumption/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"GPT-6.2 Sol","context":"## Quotas and Limits\n\nQuotas vary by account and Region. See Quotas for Amazon Bedrock and the model's service quota settings.\n\nThe output-token burndown rate is 10: each output token consumes 10 tokens of quota","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-openai-gpt-6-2-sol.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"How tokens are counted in Amazon Bedrock","context":"## Understanding token quota management\n\nWhen you make a request, tokens are deducted from your TPM and TPD quotas.\nCalculations occur at the following stages:\n\n* **At the start of the request** – The\n following sum is deducted from your quotas. The request is throttled if you\n exceed a quota.\n\n ```\n Total input tokens + max_tokens\n ```\n* **During processing** – The quota\n consumed by the request is periodically adjusted to account for the actual\n number of output tokens generated.\n* **At the end of the request** – The\n total number of tokens consumed by the request will be calculated as follows and any unused tokens are replenished to your quota:\n\n ```\n InputTokenCount + CacheWriteInputTokenCount + (OutputTokenCount x burndown rate)\n ```\n\n `CacheReadInputTokenCount` don't contribute to this calculation and are not counted toward your quota. If you don't use prompt caching, both `CacheWriteInputTokenCount` and `CacheReadInputTokenCount` will be 0.\n\n**Note:**\n\nYou're only billed for your actual token usage.\n\nFor example, if you use Anthropic Claude Sonnet 4 and send a request containing 1,000 input tokens and it generates a response equivalent to 100 tokens:\n\n* **1,500 tokens** (1,000 + 100 x 5) will be depleted from your TPM and TPD quotas.\n* You'll only be billed for **1,100 tokens**","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/quotas-token-burndown.html"}]}}

