{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock now supports observability of First Token Latency and Quota Consumption - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a managed service for building AI applications using high-performance models from leading providers, has introduced two new CloudWatch metrics: TimeToFirstToken and EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage. TimeToFirstToken measures the latency from request to first token receipt for streaming APIs (ConverseStream and InvokeModelWithResponseStream), enabling the setting of CloudWatch alarms for latency degradation and SLA baselines without client-side instrumentation. EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage tracks quota consumption, including cache write tokens and output burndown multipliers, across all inference APIs (Converse, InvokeModel, ConverseStream, and InvokeModelWithResponseStream). This metric allows proactive alarm setting before quota limits, tracking quota consumption across models, and requesting quota increases before usage is rate-limited. Both metrics are available in all commercial Bedrock regions for models accessible via cross-region inference profiles and in-region inference, updated every minute for successfully completed requests, and incur no additional cost beyond model inference usage. For more information, see the Monitoring Amazon Bedrock documentation page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/amazon-bedrock-observability-ttft-quota/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"writing-agentcore-policy","skill_description":"Authors, validates and submits Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Policy statements (Cedar and Dogwood) for an AgentCore Gateway. Discovers the Gateway's targets and tools, generates the Gateway's Cedar schema, drafts permit/forbid, guardrail and temporal policies from access requirements or from a whole natural-language policy document (splitting it into rules, marking the rules that cannot be expressed, and repairing failing drafts in batches), checks them locally (syntax, schema, API limits, claim and guardrail pitfalls), and creates or updates them in a policy engine after explicit developer approval, reporting the final status and service findings. Use when a developer wants to write, translate, fix, validate or submit AgentCore Gateway policies, convert a policy document or a list of rules into policies, restrict which users or roles can call Gateway tools, add Bedrock guardrail or session-history (temporal) conditions, or resolve CreatePolicy/UpdatePolicy failures.\n\nServices: bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: design, deploy, debug, secure\nPersona: developer, security\nWorkload: agents, generative-ai","skill_name":"writing-agentcore-policy"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"storing-and-querying-vectors","skill_description":"Store and query vector embeddings using Amazon S3 Vectors, a cost-effective long-term vector storage service with its own API namespace (s3vectors). Triggers on: create S3 vector bucket, vector index, store embeddings, semantic search, RAG vector storage, similarity search, vector database, migrate from other vector databases. Do NOT use for: querying tabular data (use querying-data-lake), S3 object storage, or hundreds/thousands of sustained QPS (use OpenSearch).\n\n\nServices: s3vectors, bedrock\nTasks: deploy, debug, migrate\nPersona: developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"storing-and-querying-vectors"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check Sonnet 5 output token burndown multiplier", "parent_id": "29c43386-6308-45f0-891f-5dd9be002573"}

